    "title": "Parking tickets getting handed out in droves along popular Colorado pass - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Spencer McKee ,\n6 hours ago People parking alongside the road on Guanella Pass. (Clear Creek County Sheriff's Office). According to the Clear Creek County Sheriff’s Office, they’ve been issuing a lot of tickets for illegal parking along Guanella Pass as of late, and leaf-peeping season isn’t even here yet.\nHome to the trailhead for the popular Mount Bierstadt and Naylor Lake, Guanella Pass gets plenty of attention throughout all months when it’s open as a thru-route, but parking illegally can mean coming back from an adventure to a bad surprise. Given the route’s narrow and steep nature, when people illegally park along the road, it quickly limits road width, which can impact the response of emergency vehicles. Because of this, authorities take illegal parking very seriously.\nOne example that highlights this issue was July 18 around noon, when portions of the road were limited to just one lane, making it difficult for everyone to travel what’s typically a two-lane route, especially those in oversized rescue or fire vehicles.\nBetween July 24 and October 5, 2025, authorities issued 127 parking tickets at a price of $87.50 each. While authorities didn’t note how much parking tickets were being issued for this year, that probably give a good idea of what type of fine violators can expect.\nInstead of parking illegally, the local sheriff’s office recommends that visitors have a back-up plan if parking in a designated area isn’t possible. Another option may be to wait in Georgetown or another nearby community and try to hit the trailhead again at a later time in the day.\nLong story, short, while illegal parking may be more convenient if one isn’t caught, this can increase the risk for everyone, from how it increases times for emergency response to how undesignated parking areas may mean parking on tall grass, thus an increased risk of starting a wildfire.\nSTAY INFORMED: Get free Colorado news with our daily newsletter (Click here)\n1",
    "summary": "According to the Clear Creek County Sheriff’s Office, they’ve been issuing a lot of tickets for illegal parking along Guanella Pass as of late, and leaf-peeping season isn’t even here yet. Home to the trailhead for the popular Mount Bierstadt and Naylor Lake, Guanella Pass gets plenty of attention throughout all months when it’s open as a thru-route, but parking illegally can mean coming back from an adventure to a bad surprise.",
